.TH AENIGMA-PROXY 8 "2026-08-10" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-proxy \- configure an Apache reverse proxy virtual host for
an Aenigma application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-proxy
.B \-d
.I DOMAIN
.B \-l
.I LOCAL_ADDRESS
.RB [ \-c
.IR CONFIG_ADDRESS ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR BIND_ADDRESS ]
.br
.B aenigma-proxy
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-proxy
installs and configures Apache HTTP Server, if not already present,
as a reverse proxy in front of a locally running ASP.NET
application, including WebSocket (SignalR) passthrough.
.PP
The script performs the following actions:
.IP \(bu 2
Installs the
.B apache2
package via
.B apt
if not already present.
.IP \(bu 2
Enables the Apache modules
.BR rewrite ,
.BR proxy ,
.BR proxy_http ,
and
.B proxy_wstunnel
required for reverse proxying and WebSocket upgrades.
.IP \(bu 2
Writes an Apache virtual host configuration to
.I /etc/apache2/sites-available/aenigma.conf
that proxies HTTP and WebSocket traffic on
.B / and /OnionRouting
to
.IR LOCAL_ADDRESS ,
transparently upgrading WebSocket connections.
.IP \(bu 2
Enables the new site with
.BR a2ensite (8)
and disables the Apache default site.
.IP \(bu 2
Restarts and enables Apache, and, if
.BR ufw (8)
is installed, opens the firewall for the
.B "Apache Full"
profile.
.IP \(bu 2
If requested via
.BR \-c ,
records the configured domain in the Aenigma application
configuration.
.PP
Each run of
.B aenigma-proxy
overwrites
.I /etc/apache2/sites-available/aenigma.conf
in full with the values given on the command line.
.PP
.B aenigma-proxy
must be run as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " DOMAIN"
The domain name the reverse proxy will serve, e.g.
.IR example.com .
Used as the
.B ServerName
of the generated virtual host. Required.
.TP
.BI \-l " LOCAL_ADDRESS"
The local address and port on which the ASP.NET application is
listening, e.g.
.IR 127.0.0.1:8080 .
All proxied HTTP and WebSocket traffic is forwarded here. Required.
.TP
.BI \-c " CONFIG_ADDRESS"
If set to
.IR 1 ,
the configured domain is recorded in the Aenigma application
configuration by invoking
.BR aenigma-config (8)
with the
.B Hostname
property, using
.IR http://DOMAIN
as the value. If set to
.I 0
or omitted, the configuration step is skipped. Optional.
.TP
.BI \-b " BIND_ADDRESS"
The interface or IP address the virtual host should bind to, e.g.
.IR 192.168.1.50 .
Used as the address portion of the generated
.B VirtualHost
directive
.RI ( BIND_ADDRESS:80 ).
If omitted, defaults to
.I *
(all interfaces), matching Apache's usual catch-all virtual host
binding. Optional.
.TP
.B \-h
Display a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The reverse proxy was configured and Apache restarted successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid or missing arguments, or the script was not run as root.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/apache2/sites-available/aenigma.conf
Generated Apache virtual host configuration; overwritten on each
run.
.TP
.I /var/log/apache2/aenigma-error.log
Apache error log for the configured virtual host.
.TP
.I /var/log/apache2/aenigma-access.log
Apache access log for the configured virtual host, using the
.B combined
log format.
.SH EXAMPLES
Configure a reverse proxy for
.I example.com
in front of a local application on port 8080:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-proxy \-d example.com \-l 127.0.0.1:8080
.EE
.RE
.PP
Bind the virtual host to a specific interface and record the
hostname in the Aenigma configuration:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-proxy \-d example.com \-l 127.0.0.1:8080 \e
    \-b 192.168.1.50 \-c 1
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Ensure that any address given via
.B \-b
is already assigned to a network interface on the host before
running the script; if Apache cannot bind to
.I BIND_ADDRESS
the subsequent restart will fail.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apache2 (8),
.BR aenigma-tor (8),
.BR aenigma-config (8),
.BR a2ensite (8),
.BR a2enmod (8),
.BR ufw (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.ruja@aenigma.ro>
